---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '~/components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '~/components/Footer.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  'datePublished': '2026-07-15',
  'dateModified': '2026-07-29',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      'headline': 'Chengdu Food Guide for First-Timers: What to Eat & How to Order',
      'description': 'Your survival guide to Sichuan cuisine. Dish-by-dish breakdown, how to order without speaking Chinese, and what to avoid.',
      'url': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/blog/chengdu-food-guide-first-timers',
      'author': {'@type': 'Organization', 'name': 'Best of Chengdu'},
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      'itemListElement': [
        {'@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 1, 'name': 'Home', 'item': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/'},
        {'@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 2, 'name': 'Blog', 'item': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/blog'},
        {'@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 3, 'name': 'Food Guide', 'item': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/blog/chengdu-food-guide-first-timers'},
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout schemaJson={JSON.stringify(schema)} title="Chengdu Food Guide for First-Timers" description="Your survival guide to Sichuan cuisine. Dish-by-dish breakdown, how to order without speaking Chinese, and what to avoid.">
  <Header />

  <main>
    <article class="max-w-[860px] mx-auto px-6 pt-20 pb-24">
      <span class="text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent font-medium">Food</span>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tighter leading-[1.1] mt-4">
        Chengdu Food Guide for First-Timers
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">You've heard Chengdu is a UNESCO City of Gastronomy. You know the food is spicy. But walking into a Chengdu restaurant for the first time can be overwhelming. Here is exactly what to eat, how to order, and how to survive &mdash; happily.</p>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">The 8 dishes you must try</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">1. Mapo Tofu (ma po dou fu)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Silky tofu cubes in a fiery, numbing sauce of doubanjiang, fermented black beans, and ground pork. The benchmark dish for any Sichuan chef. Best version: Chen Mapo Tofu near Wenshu Monastery, where it was supposedly invented in 1862. Vegetarian versions available at Buddhist restaurants.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">2. Kung Pao Chicken (gong bao ji ding)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Tender chicken cubes stir-fried with peanuts, dried chilies, and Sichuan peppercorns. The real version is a delicate balance of sweet, sour, and spicy &mdash; nothing like the syrupy takeout version. Best at Ming Ting or any mid-range Sichuan restaurant.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">3. Dan Dan Noodles (dan dan mian)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">The iconic Chengdu street dish. Thin noodles in a sauce of sesame paste, chili oil, Sichuan pepper, and minced pork. A complete meal for 12-15 RMB. Best from street stalls on Jianshe Road or at dedicated noodle shops.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">4. Twice-Cooked Pork (hui guo rou)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Pork belly simmered, sliced, then wok-fried with fermented black beans, leeks, and chili. The name is literal: it's cooked twice, and the second frying gives it a caramelized edge. Sichuan comfort food at its finest.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">5. Hotpot (huo guo)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">A communal feast of bubbling broth, fresh ingredients, and good company. Choose your broth (nine-grid spicy, mild tomato, or a split pot), order plates of raw ingredients, and cook them at the table. <a href="/food/hotpot" class="text-accent hover:underline">Full hotpot guide here</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">6. Fuqi Feipian (husband and wife lung slices)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Ignore the name &mdash; these are thin slices of beef and tripe dressed in chili oil and Sichuan pepper. Despite the intimidating description, it's one of the most popular cold appetizers in Chengdu. The numbing-spicy dressing is addictive.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">7. Zhong Dumplings (zhong shui jiao)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Pork dumplings in a sweet soy and chili oil sauce. Smaller and more delicate than northern Chinese dumplings. The balance of sweet, savory, and spicy is what makes them distinctly Chengdu.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a]">8. Bingfen (ice jelly)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">A cooling dessert made from ice jelly seeds, topped with brown sugar syrup, crushed peanuts, sesame seeds, and dried fruit. The perfect antidote to a spicy meal. Find it at street stalls and dessert shops citywide.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">How to order without speaking Chinese</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Use photos</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Most Chengdu restaurants have photo menus or dishes displayed on the wall. Point and smile works perfectly. Save photos of the dishes above to your phone as backup.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Translation apps</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Google Translate's camera mode works for printed menus. Pleco is better for handwritten signs. Take a photo, circle the text, and it translates in real time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">QR code ordering</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Many restaurants now use QR codes for ordering. Scan with WeChat or Alipay. Some have English interfaces, but even the Chinese versions have photos.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Key phrases</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">"Bu yao la" = not spicy (though this may be ignored). "Wei wei la" = a little spicy. "Hao chi" = delicious (always appreciated). "Mai dan" = the bill please.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-4">What to know about the spice</h2>
        <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          <p>Sichuan heat is different from other cuisines. It's not just chili heat (la) &mdash; it's a numbing, tingling sensation (ma) from Sichuan peppercorns. The combination is called <em>mala</em> and it's the defining flavor of the region.</p>
          <p>The numbing can be surprising. It's not pain, exactly &mdash; it's more like your mouth is vibrating. It takes a few bites to adjust. Once you do, you'll understand why people become obsessed.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">If it's too spicy:</strong> Drink milk or eat rice. Water makes it worse. The classic antidote is a bowl of plain rice or a sweet drink. Yogurt drinks sold at convenience stores also help.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Vegetarians:</strong> Sichuan has a strong Buddhist vegetarian tradition. Temple restaurants (like Wenshu Monastery) serve excellent meat-free versions of classic dishes. Many restaurants will accommodate if you say "wo chi su" (I eat vegetarian).</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">Dining etiquette</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Sharing is standard</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Dishes are placed in the center and everyone picks from them. Order one dish per person plus a soup. Rice is served individually in small bowls.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Tipping is not expected</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">No tipping culture in China. Service charges are sometimes added at high-end restaurants (check the bill). Otherwise, the price you see is the price you pay.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Table tapping</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">When someone pours tea for you, tap two fingers on the table as a silent thank-you. This tradition dates back to the Qing dynasty.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Slurping and noise</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Slurping noodles is normal and appreciated. Loud conversation is a sign of a good meal. Chengdu restaurants are lively, not hushed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>